Usage error: 'unused-everything' names no warning: the warnings are
  unused-binding, unused-function, unused-var, shadowed-update,
  unused-pattern-var, unused-parameter, unused-loop-var, and 'all'
Usage: pyk [options] <filename> [-- args]   run it with the interpreter
       pyk repl                             start the REPL
       pyk compile [options] <filename>     compile it (alias: pyk c)
       pyk notebook [--port N] [--mode M] <file.md>
                                            open a notebook in the browser
       pyk playground [--port N]            open the playground
       pyk pykhelp [--port N]               open the help in the browser
       pyk serve [--port N] [<dir>|<file>]  serve a directory's files
       pyk lsp [--stdio] [--log FILE]       the language server, for editors
       pyk dap [--stdio] [--log FILE]       the debug adapter, for editors
       pyk example [<name>]                 list the examples, or save one
       pyk highlight <file> [options]       colour code for slides: HTML, RTF
                                            or text

Options:
  -t, --target <name>   compile for <name> (default: python); with
                        `pyk <filename>`, compile for it and run it
  -o, --output <file>   name the executable (default: the source's
                        name); what is generated goes in build/ beside
                        it
  -L, --lib-path <dir>  a directory of libraries to import from; repeat
                        for several
  --stdout              print the generated code instead of writing it
  --run                 build and run what was compiled, with the
                        target's own commands
  --release             compile in release mode: every call to debug()
                        is removed, and print() is kept
  -W<name>, -Wno-<name> turn one warning on or off; -Wall turns every
                        one on and -w every one off (`pyk --help` lists
                        them)
  -Werror[=<name>]      make every warning an error, or just that one;
                        -Wno-error[=<name>] undoes it
  --use <feature>       with the REPL, turn a feature on from the start,
                        as a 'use' line would: mutation or objects
  --tinyweb-log         with the interpreter or the REPL, print what a
                        program sends its tinyweb page, and what the page
                        answers, on stderr as the page runs
  --port <n>            serve the notebook, the playground, the help
                        or a directory on port <n>
  --mode <mode>         the notebook for a student (the default) or a
                        teacher, or test: check it instead of serving it
  --                    everything after it is the program's own
                        arguments, read by sys.args()
  --version             print the version and exit
  --help                this, in full: the rest of the flags, what each
                        command does, and what each target writes

Targets:
python, node, web, iweb, c, java, ocaml, pyking -- each its family's
  default version; `pyk --help` lists the versions.
exit 1
